WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.980
In this video, you'll learn about some of the classic neural network architectures,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.820
starting with Lynet 5, and then AlexNet, and then VGGNet.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.820
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.020
Here's the Lynet 5 architecture.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:20.820
You start off with an image, which say 32x32x1, and the goal of Lynet 5 was to recognize handwritten

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.620
digits, so maybe an image of a digit like that.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:32.300
And Lynet 5 was trained on grayscale images, which is why it's 32x32x1.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:37.700
This neural network architecture is actually quite similar to the last example you saw last

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:38.700
week.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.900
In the first step, you use a set of six 5x5 filters with a stride of 1.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:51.100
Because you use six filters, you end up with a 28x28x6 over there, and with a stride of

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:59.220
1 and no padding, the image dimensions reduces from 32x32 down to 28x28.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.900
Then the Lynet neural network applies pooling.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.780
And back then, when this paper was written, people used average pooling much more.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:12.420
If you're building a modern variant, you'd probably use max pooling instead.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.580
But in this example, your average pool, and with a filter width of 2 and a stride of

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.860
2, you wind up reducing the dimensions to height and width by a factor of 2.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.940
So you now end up with a 14x14x6 volume.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.380
I guess the height and width of these volumes aren't entirely drawn to scale.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.780
Technically, if I were drawing these volumes to scale, the height and width would be shrunk

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:37.980
by a factor of 2.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.420
Next, you apply another convolutional layer.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:47.180
This time, you use a set of 16 filters to a 5x5, so you end up with 16 channels in

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.220
the next volume.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:54.980
And back when this paper was written in 1998, people didn't really use padding, or you're

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:00.020
always using valid convolutions, which is why every time you apply a convolutional layer,

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.140
the height and width shrinks.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:06.900
So that's why here you go from 14x14 down to 10x10.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.220
Then another pooling layer, so that it reduces the height and width by a factor of 2, and

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.140
you end up with 5x5 over here.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:20.660
And if you multiply out these numbers, 5x5 by 16, this multiplies out to 400.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.300
That's 25 times 16 is 400.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:32.900
And the next layer is then a fully connected layer that fully connects each of these 400

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.940
nodes with every one of 120 neurons.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.820
So there's a fully connected layer.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:45.620
And sometimes I would draw out explicitly a layer with 400 nodes, but I'm skipping that

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.620
here.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.700
There's a fully connected layer.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.820
And then another fully connected layer.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:56.220
And then the final step is it uses these essentially 84 features and uses it with one

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.220
final output.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.740
I guess you could draw one more node here to make a prediction for y hat.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:07.980
And y hat took on 10 possible values corresponding to recognizing each of the digits from 0

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:08.980
to 9.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:16.580
A modern version of this neural network would use a softmax layer with a 10-way classification

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.580
output.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:23.540
Although back then, the net5 actually used a different classifier at the output layer,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.640
one that's useless today.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:32.820
So this neural network was small by modern standards, had about 60,000 parameters.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:39.780
And today you often see neural networks with anywhere from 10 million to 100 million parameters.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.060
And it's not unusual to see networks that are literally about a thousand times bigger

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.500
than this network.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.700
But one thing you do see is that as you go deeper in the networks, as you go from

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:55.260
left to right, the height and width tend to go down.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.020
So you went from 30 by 32 to 28 to 14 to 10 to 5.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.900
Whereas the number of channels tends to increase.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:11.420
It goes from 1 to 6 to 16 as you go deeper into the layers of the network.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.220
One other pattern you see in this neural network that's still often repeated today is

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:20.980
that you might have some one or more conf layers followed by a pulling layer and then

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:26.260
one, or sometimes more than one conf layer followed by a pulling layer and then some

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.220
fully connected layers and then the output.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:34.140
So this type of arrangement of layers is quite common.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:38.660
Now finally, this is maybe only for those of you that want to try reading the paper.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.940
There are a couple other things that were different.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:47.820
The rest of this slide, I'm going to make a few more advanced comments only for those

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.380
of you that want to try to read this classic paper.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:58.140
And so everything I'm going to write in red, you can safely skip on this slide.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:02.620
And this may be an interesting historical footnote that is okay if you don't follow

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.540
fully.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:10.140
So it turns out that if you read the original paper, back then people used sigmoid and

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:16.420
tanh nonlinearities and people weren't using ReLU nonlinearities back then.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:20.740
So if you look at the paper, you see sigmoid and tanh referred to.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:25.700
And there are also some funny ways about this network was wired, at least funny by

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:26.940
modern standards.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:35.380
So for example, you've seen how if you have a NH by NW by NC network with NC channels,

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:42.940
then you use F by F by the same NC dimensional filter where every filter looks at every one

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:43.940
of these channels.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:47.380
But back then, computers were much slower.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:52.380
And so to save on computation as well as on parameters, the original Linet 5 had some

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:57.100
crazy complicated way where different filters would look at different channels of the input

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.380
block.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:03.020
And so the paper talks about those details, but the more modern implementation wouldn't

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:07.180
have that type of complexity these days.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:12.020
And then one last thing that was done back then, I guess, but isn't really done

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:19.940
right now is that the original Linet 5 had a nonlinearity after pooling.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:25.140
And I think it actually uses sigmoid nonlinearity after the pooling layer.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.780
So if you do read this paper, and this is one of the harder ones to read, so the ones

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:34.060
will go over in the next few videos, the next one might be an easier one to start

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.060
with.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:40.260
Most of the ideas on this slide are described in sections two and three of the paper.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:44.740
In later sections of the paper talk about some other ideas.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:48.980
It talked about something called a draft transformer network, which isn't widely used

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:49.980
today.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:54.420
So if you do try to read this paper, I recommend focusing on really on section two, which talks

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:59.300
about this architecture, and maybe take a quick look at section three, which has

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.760
a bunch of experimental results, which are pretty interesting.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:07.060
The second example of a neural network I want to show you is AlexNet, named after Alex

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:12.620
Trezewski, who was the first author of the paper describing this work.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.980
The other authors were Elias Suskiver and Jeffrey Hinton.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.740
So AlexNet input starts with 227 by 227 by 3 images.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:27.140
Oh, and if you read the paper, the paper refers to 224 by 224 by 3 images.

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:31.620
But if you look at the numbers, I think the numbers make sense only of actually 227 by

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.620
227.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:40.420
And then the first layer applies a set of 96 11 by 11 filters with a stride of 4.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:45.940
And because it uses a large stride of 4, the dimensions shrinks to 55 by 55.

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:51.640
So roughly going down by a factor of 4 because of the last stride.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:55.220
And then it applies max pooling with a 3 by 3 filter.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:58.200
So f equals 3 and a stride of 2.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:05.400
So this reduces the volume to 27 by 27 by 96.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.640
And then it performs a 5 by 5 same convolution.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:09.640
So we're padding.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:15.320
So you end up with 27 by 27 by 276.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:20.360
Max pooling again, this then reduces the height and width to 13.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:24.000
And then another same convolution, so same padding.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.920
So it's 13 by 13 by now, 384 filters.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.720
And then 3 by 3, same convolution again, gives you that.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:40.040
Then 3 by 3, same convolution gives you that.

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:45.520
Then max pool brings it down to 6 by 6 by 256.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:52.200
If you multiply out these numbers, 6 times 6 times 256, that's 9,216.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:57.320
So we're going to unroll this into 9,216 nodes.

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:01.440
And then finally, it has a few fully connected layers.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:08.160
And then finally, it uses a softmax to output which one of 1,000 classes the object could

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:09.800
be.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:17.720
So this neural network actually had a lot of similarities to the net.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:21.160
But it was much bigger.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:28.120
So whereas the net or the net 5 from the previous slide had 60,000, about 60,000 parameters,

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:32.720
this AlexNet had about 60 million parameters.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:37.800
And the fact that it could take pretty similar basic building blocks, but have a lot more

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.760
hidden units and train it on a lot more data, so you trained on the ImageNet dataset that

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:46.600
allowed it to have just remarkable performance.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:51.600
Another aspect of this architecture that made it much better than the net was using the

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.840
ReLU activation function.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:58.600
And then again, just if you read the paper, some more advanced details that you don't

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:01.160
really need to worry about if you don't read the paper.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:06.160
One is that when this paper was written, GPUs were still a little bit slower.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:11.440
So it had a complicated way of training on two GPUs.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:17.240
And the basic idea was that a lot of these layers was actually split across two different

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:23.800
GPUs, and there was a thoughtful way for when the two GPUs would communicate with each other.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:31.600
And the paper also, the original AlexNet architecture also had another type of layer called a local

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.240
response normalization.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:39.000
And this type of layer isn't really used much, which is why I didn't talk about it.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:45.240
But the basic idea of local response normalization is if you look at one of these blocks, one of

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:49.920
these volumes that we have on top, let's say for the sake of argument, this one, 13 by

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.520
13 by 256.

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:58.640
What local response normalization, LRN, does is you'll look at one position, so one position

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:05.080
of height and width, and look down this across all the channels, look at all 256 numbers

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:07.280
and normalize them.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:12.120
And the motivation for this local response normalization was that for each position in

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:20.320
this 13 by 13 image, maybe you don't want too many neurons with a very high activation.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:24.720
But subsequently, many researchers have found that this doesn't help that much.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:30.040
So this is one of those ideas that I guess I'm drawing in red, because it's less important

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:32.000
for you to understand this one.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:37.640
And in practice, I don't really use local response normalizations really in the networks

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:38.640
that I would train today.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:44.080
So if you're interested in the history of deep learning, I think even before AlexNet,

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:49.160
deep learning was starting to gain traction and speech recognition and a few other areas.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:54.960
But it was really this paper that convinced a lot of the computer vision community to

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:58.520
take a serious look at deep learning and to convince them that deep learning really

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:00.120
works in computer vision.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:04.240
And then it grew on to have a huge impact, not just in computer vision, but beyond

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:05.520
computer vision as well.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:09.920
And if you want to try reading some of these papers yourself, and you really don't have

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:15.040
to for this course, but if you want to try reading some of these papers, this one is

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:16.760
one of the easier ones to read.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:19.360
So this might be a good one to take a look at.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:24.160
So whereas AlexNet has a relatively complicated architecture, there are just a lot of

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:31.240
hyper parameters, right, where you have all these numbers that Alex Truszewski and

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:33.320
his co-authors had to come up with.

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:38.880
Let me show you a third and final example in this video called the VGG or the VGG 16

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:39.920
network.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:46.000
And a remarkable thing about the VGG 16 is that they said, instead of having so many

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:51.520
hyper parameters, let's use a much simpler network where you focus on just having

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:57.760
conv layers that are just 3 by 3 filters with a stride of 1 and always use the same

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:03.520
padding and make all your max pooling layers 2 by 2 with a stride of 2.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:10.120
And so one very nice thing about the VGG network was it really simplified these

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:12.160
neural network architectures.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:14.440
So let's go through the architecture.

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:19.640
So you start off with an image and then the first two layers are convolutions,

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:24.520
which are therefore these 3 by 3 filters.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:27.800
And in the first layers, first two layers, you use 64 filters.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:34.960
So you end up with a 224 by 224 because using same convolutions and then with 64

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:35.960
channels.

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:41.440
So because VGG 16 is a relatively deep network, I'm going to not draw all the

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:42.280
volumes here.

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:47.600
So what this little picture denotes is what we would previously have drawn as this

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:58.120
224 by 224 by 3 and then a convolution that results in I guess a 224 by 224 by 64.

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:00.760
This would be drawn as a deeper volume.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:07.440
And then another layer that results in 224 by 224 by 64.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:15.160
So this conv64 times 2 represents that you're doing two layers, two conv layers with 64

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:16.160
filters.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:22.480
And as I mentioned earlier, the filters are always 3 by 3 with a stride of 1 and they're

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:24.560
always same convolutions.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:27.920
So rather than drawing all these volumes, I'm just going to use text to represent this

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:28.920
network.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.800
Next, then uses a pooling layer.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:35.960
So the pooling layer will reduce and think about it, it goes from 224 by 224 down to

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:36.960
what?

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:41.080
Right, it goes to 112 by 112 by 64.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:44.300
And then it has a couple more conv layers.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:48.820
So this means it has a 128 filters.

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:52.100
And because these are the same convolutions, let's see, what's the new dimension?

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:56.820
Right, it will be 112 by 112 by 128.

00:14:56.820 --> 00:15:01.140
And then pooling layer, so you can figure out what's the new dimension, right?

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:02.340
It will be that.

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:09.020
And now three conv layers with 256 dimensional filters, 256 filters, sorry.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:17.540
Then the pooling layer and then a few more conv layers, pooling layer, more conv layers,

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.700
pooling layer.

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:27.220
And then it takes this final 7 by 7 by 512, feeds it to fully connected layer, fully

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:36.540
connected with 4096 units and then a softmax output, one of a thousand classes.

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:43.500
By the way, the 16 in the name VGG16 refers to the fact that this has 16 layers that have

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:45.420
weights.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:47.600
And this is a pretty large network.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:52.820
This network has a total of about 138 million parameters.

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:55.940
And that's pretty large, even by modern standards.

00:15:55.940 --> 00:16:00.820
But the simplicity of the VGG16 architecture made it quite appealing.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:03.700
You can tell this architecture is really quite uniform.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:08.420
There's a few conv layers followed by a pooling layer which reduces the height and

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:09.700
width.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:14.100
So the pooling layers reduce the height and width.

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:15.780
You have a few of them here.

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:20.780
But then also, if you look at the number of filters in the conv layers, here you have

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:28.660
64 filters and then you double to 128, double to 256, double to 512.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:33.220
And then I guess the oldest factor was big enough and didn't double the game here.

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:38.500
But this, you know, sort of roughly doubling on every step or doubling through every

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:45.020
stack of conv layers was another simple principle used to design the architecture of this network.

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:51.020
And so I think the relative uniformity of this architecture made it quite attractive

00:16:51.020 --> 00:16:52.420
to researchers.

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:56.740
The main downside was that it was a pretty large network in terms of the number of

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:59.140
parameters you had to train.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:04.580
Oh, and if you read the literature, you sometimes see people talk about VGG19.

00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:08.780
That's an even bigger version of this network.

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:14.220
And you could see the details in the paper cited at the bottom by Karen Simeon and

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:16.460
Andrew Zissimann.

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:23.660
But because VGG16 does almost as well as VGG19, a lot of people will use VGG16.

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:28.660
But the thing I liked most about this was that this made this pattern of how, as you

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:31.060
go deeper, height and width goes down.

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:35.060
It just goes down by a factor of two each time for the pooling layers, whereas the

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:36.860
number of channels increases.

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:41.860
And here it, you know, roughly goes up by a factor of two every time you have a new

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:42.860
set of conv layers.

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:49.220
So by making the rate at which these goes down and that go up very systematic, I

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:54.660
thought this paper was very, very attractive from that perspective.

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:57.900
So that's it for the three classic architectures.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:00.900
If you want, you should now read some of these papers.

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:05.420
I recommend starting with the AlexNet paper, followed by the VGGNet paper, and then

00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:09.060
the Lynet paper is a bit harder to read, but it is a good classic if you want to

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:10.180
take a look at that.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:14.300
But next, let's go beyond these classic networks and look at some even more

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:17.500
advanced, even more powerful neural network architectures.

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:18.740
Let's go on to the next video.
